Potential Memory Leak with INotifyPropertyChanged and Command Binding in WPF

I have the following class structure in a WPF application:

class A {
    public B BProperty { get; set; }

    public A()
    {
        BProperty = new B();
    }
}

class B : INotifyPropertyChanged {

    public string Display { get; set; }

    public event PropertyChangedEventHandler PropertyChanged;

    public ICommand DoSomethingCommand { get; set; }

    ....
}

In my WPF view, I'm using class A as a DataContext and binding a button's command to the DoSomethingCommand of the BProperty:

<Button Command="{Binding BProperty.DoSomethingCommand}"></Button>

The class A will be initialized on a Window open event.

I was getting a memory leak when opening and closing the Window multiple times.

The problem was that class A doesn't inherit the "INotifyPropertyChanged" interface, while class B does.

But I don't know why that can be a problem. Could someone explain to me the issue here?
